# Use Flutter for Mobile Application

## Context and Problem Statement
We need a cross-platform mobile application for real-time hand gesture recognition to control the TurtleBot3 robot. The app must access the device camera, run MediaPipe hand landmark detection on-device, and communicate gestures via WebSocket to the ROS2 backend.

## Decision Drivers
* Must support iOS and Android from single codebase
* Needs efficient camera access and image processing
* Must integrate MediaPipe for hand tracking
* Requires WebSocket communication support
* Should enable rapid UI development
* Performance critical for real-time gesture detection

## Considered Options
* Flutter
* React Native
* Native iOS (Swift) + Android (Kotlin)

## Decision Outcome
Chosen option: **Flutter**, because it provides native performance, excellent camera plugin ecosystem, official MediaPipe support, and enables us to maintain one codebase for both iOS and Android platforms.

### Consequences
* Good, because we write once and deploy to both iOS and Android
* Good, because Flutter has mature camera and WebSocket packages
* Good, because MediaPipe has official Flutter/Dart support
* Good, because hot reload speeds up UI development
* Bad, because team needs to learn Dart programming language
* Bad, because app size is larger than native apps

## Pros and Cons of Options

### Flutter
* Good: Single codebase for iOS and Android
* Good: Native performance (compiled to native code)
* Good: Official MediaPipe Tasks package available
* Good: Mature camera plugin (`camera` package)
* Good: WebSocket support (`web_socket_channel`)
* Good: Fast development with hot reload
* Bad: Dart language learning curve
* Bad: Larger app bundle size

### React Native
* Good: JavaScript/TypeScript (more familiar)
* Good: Large community
* Bad: MediaPipe integration less mature
* Bad: Bridge performance overhead for camera processing
* Bad: More platform-specific code needed

### Native Development
* Good: Maximum performance and control
* Good: Direct platform API access
* Bad: Need to maintain separate iOS and Android codebases
* Bad: Duplicated development effort
* Bad: Harder to keep features in sync
